WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.840
very big news that I want to couch in some
factual realities.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.980
Joe Biden yesterday signed an executive order
directing the Justice Department to end its

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.839
use of private prisons.

00:00:12.839 --> 00:00:20.700
Joe Biden yesterday telling the Justice Department
and the reliance on private prisons acknowledge

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:26.870
the central role that the federal government
has played in implementing discriminatory

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.610
housing policies.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:33.079
And Joe Biden made some remarks before signing
these orders saying that the US government

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:36.489
needs a completely new approach to the issue
of racial equity.

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:41.359
He said the country is less prosperous and
less secure because of systemic racism, saying

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:42.429
we must change now.

00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:45.110
It will take time, but we can do it.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:51.979
And Biden called on the Justice Department
to curb the use of private prisons right away.

00:00:51.979 --> 00:00:55.069
Now, I think that this is very important.

00:00:55.069 --> 00:01:01.729
We've talked before about private prisons
versus public prisons, but there are a couple

00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:02.760
caveats here.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.030
And I want to be really clear.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.119
I'm not raining on Joe Biden's parade, but
what I want people to understand is the reality

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:10.659
of the numbers.

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:18.550
First of all, in the United States, most inmates
are held in state facilities, not federal

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:19.550
facilities.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:26.460
So in general, when we're talking about federal
prisons, it's a slice, a relatively small

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.219
one of all inmates.

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:36.079
Generally speaking, if you look only at private
prisons, most private prison inmates are in

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:37.939
state private prisons.

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:44.340
And in fact, just a few years ago, if you
look at all inmates in private prisons, 78

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.450
percent of them are in state private prisons.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:52.820
Only 22 percent of private prison inmates
are in federal private prisons.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:59.270
So the vast majority of inmates and the vast
majority of private prison inmates are state

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:04.469
inmates who would not be in any way directly
affected by what Joe Biden is saying.

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:09.750
Now, that being said, it is still very important
that Joe Biden is doing this.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.610
Number one, the Bureau of Prisons, Federal
Bureau of Prisons recently opted not to renew

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:21.019
some private prison contracts in the recent
months as the number of inmates has been dwindling

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:25.790
and many were released to home confinement
because of the coronavirus pandemic.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:29.230
This is the perfect time to say let's keep
going in that direction.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.980
And furthermore, there's no doubt that there
are many states that will take the lead from

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.049
the federal government.

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:38.390
When the federal government says at the federal
level for federal inmates, we are going to

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.190
get away from private prisons.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:46.040
Certainly at least the more blue states and
maybe purple states would see that as a signal

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.690
as the door opening for them to do the exact
same thing.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.880
So I do think that this is extraordinarily
important going forward.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.780
There's going to be there's another issue
that follows this, which is that there are

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:07.540
many private prisons that have occupancy minimums
where essentially they have a contract with

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.280
the state or federal government.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.770
It's mostly state, but they're state and federal
where they say, OK, we will run this prison

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.409
for you, but you need to guarantee us a certain
occupancy rate.

00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:23.810
If you drop below that occupancy rate, you
still have to pay us as if we were that full.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:30.200
And what that generates is a perverse incentive
to lower the bar at which you lock people

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.200
up.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.470
If we have a 92 percent occupancy requirement
with this prison, but really we're only using

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:41.880
85 percent, there's money we're paying as
if we were locking people up, even though

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.420
there's nobody locked up.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.950
Let's find some people to lock up so we get
our so-called money's worth from these contracts.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.000
It's completely perverse.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.030
It's it could not be a more backward system.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.410
So if we are, this is a great first step.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:55.530
We're on day seven.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:04:00.799
Yesterday was day six for Joe Biden or I guess
the start of day seven yesterday for Joe Biden.

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:01.799
This is a great start.

00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:03.920
There's no question about it.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.079
But if we really want.

00:04:05.079 --> 00:04:10.620
To permanently reduce incarceration, it's
not just about doing away with the contracts

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:18.120
with private prisons, we also need to change
the approach as to who do we even try to put?

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.549
Who do prosecutors even ask for prison time
for?

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:25.590
That's obviously a much bigger issue and it's
one that we've talked about before and we'll

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:26.840
continue to talk about.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.400
But I agree completely with Joe Biden in doing
this.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.030
Let's hope some states follow suit and let's
hope we actually deal with, you know, there

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:41.510
is a trickle down effect here, which is what
kind of policing do we do?

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:48.380
Who gets arrested for what crimes, who gets
charged, what penalties and sentences are

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:49.420
requested.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:55.630
All of that stuff runs downhill, so to speak,
and ends up with what number of people do

00:04:55.630 --> 00:05:00.960
we have in prisons and what level of demand
do we have for bunks or cells, I guess is

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.259
what we would call them.

00:05:02.259 --> 00:05:05.990
And you've got to start at the top and allow
those changes to trickle down.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.870
Joe Biden here sort of starting towards the
bottom, but that's OK.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.960
And hopefully he will do much more.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.690
We'll have more about this on our Instagram
page.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.310
Find us on Instagram at The David Pakman Show,
The David Pakman Show at David Pakman Dotcom.

